102
00:03:57,920 --> 00:04:02,600
All right. So, here is Landsberg's story.

103
00:04:02,600 --> 00:04:05,480
All right. So, let's start with a really simple case

104
00:04:05,480 --> 00:04:08,640
where we've got shirts where

105
00:04:08,640 --> 00:04:11,240
there's an $18 marginal cost producing them,

106
00:04:11,240 --> 00:04:14,840
and then you are willing to pay $20 for a shirt.

107
00:04:14,840 --> 00:04:17,520
All right. And there's no taxes, let's start off with.

108
00:04:17,520 --> 00:04:19,200
So, there's a point of competition.

109
00:04:19,200 --> 00:04:22,640
So, you value a shirt for $20,

110
00:04:22,640 --> 00:04:25,040
they're sold for $18, do you buy the shirt?

111
00:04:25,040 --> 00:04:28,200
Sure. Right? And when you buy the shirt,

112
00:04:28,200 --> 00:04:29,720
how much consumer surplus do you get?

113
00:04:29,720 --> 00:04:31,960
Two dollars. And how much revenue does

114
00:04:31,960 --> 00:04:33,840
the government get when the tax is zero?

115
00:04:33,840 --> 00:04:36,280
Zero. All right. Now, suppose the government

116
00:04:36,280 --> 00:04:38,080
goes and imposes a one cent tax.

117
00:04:38,080 --> 00:04:40,240
Now, the price is $19.01.

118
00:04:40,240 --> 00:04:41,240
Do you still buy the shirt?

119
00:04:41,240 --> 00:04:43,880
Yes. Yes. What consumer surplus do you get?

120
00:04:43,880 --> 00:04:45,200
$0.99.

121
00:04:45,200 --> 00:04:48,200
$1.99. And how much tax revenue

122
00:04:48,200 --> 00:04:49,480
does the government raise off of you?

123
00:04:49,480 --> 00:04:51,040
One cent. One cent. All right.

124
00:04:51,040 --> 00:04:52,320
Fine. The government puts a dollar tax.

125
00:04:52,320 --> 00:04:53,800
How much consumer surplus do you get?

126
00:04:53,800 --> 00:04:55,440
One. One. A dollar. How much tax revenue

127
00:04:55,440 --> 00:04:56,680
does the government get? A dollar.

128
00:04:56,680 --> 00:04:59,440
Government imposes a $1.99 tax on the shirt.

129
00:04:59,440 --> 00:05:01,280
How much can you still buy?

130
00:05:01,280 --> 00:05:03,520
Yes. Sure. And how much surplus do you get?

131
00:05:03,520 --> 00:05:04,960
A penny. A penny. How much tax revenue

132
00:05:04,960 --> 00:05:06,880
does the government get? $1.99.

133
00:05:06,880 --> 00:05:08,840
All right. So, so far, this is so obvious,

134
00:05:08,840 --> 00:05:10,040
you may wonder what the point is.

135
00:05:10,040 --> 00:05:11,600
Then the government goes and imposes

136
00:05:11,600 --> 00:05:13,720
a $2.01 tax.

137
00:05:13,720 --> 00:05:15,720
The price is now $20.01.

138
00:05:15,720 --> 00:05:16,880
Do you still buy?

139
00:05:16,880 --> 00:05:19,960
No. No. How much consumer surplus do you get?

140
00:05:19,960 --> 00:05:21,760
Zero. None. How much revenue

141
00:05:21,760 --> 00:05:22,720
does the government raise?

142
00:05:22,720 --> 00:05:25,120
Zero. None. And that's what

143
00:05:25,120 --> 00:05:27,120
deadweight costs are all about.

144
00:05:27,120 --> 00:05:31,040
So, as long as all the government doing,

145
00:05:31,040 --> 00:05:33,320
doing is raising the, raising,

146
00:05:33,320 --> 00:05:35,400
raising the tax without changing behavior,

147
00:05:35,400 --> 00:05:38,200
it's just shifting the surplus from you to itself.

148
00:05:38,200 --> 00:05:40,360
But if you raise it enough,

149
00:05:40,360 --> 00:05:42,320
then the result is not that it shifts

150
00:05:42,320 --> 00:05:44,240
even more from you to itself,

151
00:05:44,240 --> 00:05:46,320
but that it destroys all of your surplus

152
00:05:46,320 --> 00:05:48,360
and gets no surplus for itself.

153
00:05:48,360 --> 00:05:52,800
And that's why taxes are bad in economic terms.

154
00:05:52,800 --> 00:05:54,000
That's the whole problem.

155
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:56,040
And that's, and again, of course,

156
00:05:56,040 --> 00:05:58,720
the same goes for, if this were a monopolist.

157
00:05:58,720 --> 00:06:00,640
So if there's a monopolist that

158
00:06:00,640 --> 00:06:02,120
we're going and starts up by

159
00:06:02,120 --> 00:06:04,120
charging marginal cost and it gets no surplus,

160
00:06:04,120 --> 00:06:05,520
you get it all, if it raises

161
00:06:05,520 --> 00:06:07,480
the price on the unit that you're buying,

162
00:06:07,480 --> 00:06:09,920
then it takes away more and more of your surplus,

163
00:06:09,920 --> 00:06:11,120
you're getting less and less.

164
00:06:11,120 --> 00:06:13,400
But then finally it reaches the breaking point

165
00:06:13,400 --> 00:06:15,160
where it's beyond your willingness to pay.

166
00:06:15,160 --> 00:06:17,760
And then you say, I don't want it anymore.

167
00:06:17,760 --> 00:06:20,480
And then the firm gets nothing.

168
00:06:20,480 --> 00:06:23,040
And that's the nature of

169
00:06:23,040 --> 00:06:24,280
allocated efficiency and that's

170
00:06:24,280 --> 00:06:25,760
what this triangle is all about.

171
00:06:25,760 --> 00:06:29,400
This is all the surplus that is lost without it

172
00:06:29,400 --> 00:06:31,160
being a loss to consumers without

173
00:06:31,160 --> 00:06:33,800
being gained by the producers.

174
00:06:35,240 --> 00:06:38,560
All right. So, questions.

175
00:06:38,560 --> 00:06:42,120
Yes. How does that work with things like

176
00:06:42,120 --> 00:06:46,600
syntaxes where you're on the books,

177
00:06:46,600 --> 00:06:50,160
you're making it just to push people away from it?

178
00:06:50,160 --> 00:06:52,000
Ah, yeah, yeah. So, like,

179
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:53,560
if it's something with a negative externality,

